<p>
Each <code>config/tradelists/trader-&lt;specialization&gt;.json</code> (and
<code>villager-&lt;name&gt;.json</code>) file is a flat <code>{money, selling, buying}</code>
object; entries repeating the same item under different cosmetic <code>attributes</code> (e.g. a
tapestry listed many times under different "type" values) are collapsed to one listing, since
those variations are randomized/cosmetic rather than distinct tradeable things.
</p>
<p>
There's no explicit field anywhere linking a specialization to the trader entity that actually
uses it -- <code>entities/humanoid/trader-{male,female}.json</code>'s own <code>variantgroups</code>
declares a <code>type</code> dimension whose states <em>are</em> the specialization codes, and the
entity's <code>attributes.tradePropsFile</code> substitutes that same value into the tradelist
filename. Every concrete entity variant whose <code>type</code> matches a loaded specialization's
code is linked back to it; the 8 <code>villager-*</code> tradelists intentionally resolve to no
entity, since named villagers aren't assigned a specialization through this mechanism at all.
</p>
